      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-bdddeb99cc17cb63\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.9,\"analysis\":\"The quoted formula denotes the truth-conditional avowal that one loves the addressee, but it is embedded under “Nor can I say” and is therefore not actually asserted to the beloved at this point. The passage invokes that ordinary avowal in order to reject its subject–object structure as inadequate to Birkin’s imagined state of transcendent union. There is no independent undertaking force or evidence that the words function as affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The surrounding narration says the experience is “beyond love” and makes the ordinary formula inadequate, but this is a philosophical rejection of the hypothetical avowal’s applicability, not a distinct core force requiring O.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"hypothetical\",\"description\":\"A contemplated but explicitly disallowed utterance embedded in “Nor can I say ‘I love you’”; the phrase is cited as the ordinary avowal that would fail to express the described oneness.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Birkin’s perspective treats “I love you” as dependent on separate identities—an I speaking to a you—whereas his bliss is narrated as the dissolution of both into a silent “oneness.” The phrase therefore remains intelligible as a love-state avowal, but the narration says it cannot capture the allegedly supra-personal experience. This follows actual whispered declarations that are called truthful yet “not the real truth,” emphasizing representational inadequacy rather than performative commitment or reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Nor can I say ‘I love you,’ when I have ceased to be, and you have ceased to be”\",\"supports\":\"Establishes that the occurrence is a hypothetical or disallowed avowal rather than a direct utterance to the beloved.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Speech travels between the separate parts. But in the perfect One there is perfect silence of bliss.”\",\"supports\":\"Explains why the conventional truth-conditional formula is judged inapplicable: it presupposes distinct speaker and addressee.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, his truthful “I love you, I love you” is described as “not the real truth” because the experience was “something beyond love.”\",\"supports\":\"Shows that the narrative contrasts the ordinary avowal with a purportedly transcendent experience; it does not recast the words as a commitment or reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is in D. H. Lawrence’s Women in Love, Chapter XXVII, and is tagged as an affirmative present-tense explicit love formula.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the bibliographic location and conventional grammatical form of the embedded clause.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E/O adequately represents the core: T captures the invoked avowal, while its hypothetical negation and philosophical inadequacy are handled by utterance status and context. No residual core force warrants O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
